  "answer": "The study confirmed only four of the five cases it was looking for, one on each of four scenes: 048b974e at 0.6x / \u22121.5 s (S1), 07981e6a at 1.2x / +2 s (S6), 0d4893f5 at 1.2x / +1 s (S16) and 1bbe02fc at 0.6x / \u22121 s (S28). Each confirmed setting failed on every kept run, but there were only a few repeats at each, so the 90% ranges only show the failure rate is above one half. They do not show it is near certain. None of these failures involved the pedestrian. In every triaged failure, the policy steered left of its route, often ignoring a RIGHT command. It then hit a parked, oncoming or neighbouring vehicle, failed to brake for a slow car ahead, or left the road. So these settings answer \"where does the policy fail\" more than \"which speed and pedestrian timing cause a pedestrian conflict\". All of S1's failures were flagged as possible simulator artifacts because the ego was far off the recorded path. Triage still puts them on the policy's wrong turn.",
